<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place</p><h1 class="enc-title">Botswana</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Botswana is a landlocked country in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, much of it covered by the Kalahari Desert, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okavango-delta">Okavango Delta</a> in the north-west. Its Tswana <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a> came under British protection as the Bechuanaland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorate</a> in 1885, and it became independent in 1966 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seretse-khama">Seretse Khama</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaborone">Gaborone</a>. The discovery of diamonds soon after independence helped make it one of Africa's most stable and prosperous democracies.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/botswana.jpg" alt="Botswana" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SKopp, Gabbe, Madden · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Botswana.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Kingdom of Mapungubwe arose in the valley where the Shashe River joins the Limpopo, today the meeting point of South Africa, <b>Botswana</b> and Zimbabwe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mapungubwe">Kingdom of Mapungubwe · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The war made the country a Cold War theatre: ZIPRA drew arms and training from the Soviet Union, ZANLA from China, and both from FRELIMO's Mozambique and from Zambia and <b>Botswana</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rhodesia">Rhodesia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Mellet holds that a proto-Khoe people emerged between 400 BCE and 100 CE in southern Zimbabwe, and also in northern <b>Botswana</b> and along the Limpopo, as the San peoples Tshua and Khwe met herding communities drifting slowly south from East Africa.</blockquote><a href="/culture/khoekhoe">Khoekhoe · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>In <b>Botswana</b> they are called Basarwa.</blockquote><a href="/culture/san-people">San people · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>…survived colonial rule across Africa and the Pacific, and many states after independence gave it a formal place: <b>Botswana</b>’s Ntlo ya Dikgosi, the House of Chiefs, Ghana’s National House of Chiefs and South Africa’s houses of traditional leaders advise elected governments, and Sierra Leone’s local government rests on…</blockquote><a href="/government/chiefdom">Chiefdom · Strengths</a></li><li><blockquote>Reassembled under Moilwa in the Marico valley, they came under the rule of the Transvaal Boer republic after 1852, though Hurutshe communities persist around Zeerust and in <b>Botswana</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/bahurutshe-chiefdom">Bahurutshe Chiefdom</a></li></ul></section>
